        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.815,
            0.05,
            0.861,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Area"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.087,
            0.682,
            0.126
          ],
          "content": "What can we say about the lengths of AB and its reflection $\\mathrm { A B ^ { \\prime } } ?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.13,
            0.447,
            0.15
          ],
          "content": "Since $\\Delta \\mathrm{AXB} \\cong \\Delta \\mathrm{AXB}', \\Delta \\mathrm{B} = \\mathrm{AB}'$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.155,
            0.341,
            0.174
          ],
          "content": "Similarly, $\\mathrm{AC} = \\mathrm{AC}'$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.178,
            0.682,
            0.217
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, the length of the path $\\mathrm { B } \\to \\mathrm { A } \\to \\mathrm { C }$ is the same as the length of the path $\\overline { { \\mathsf { B } } }   \\xrightarrow { }   \\mathsf { A }   \\xrightarrow { }   \\mathsf { C } ^ { \\prime }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.178,
            0.228,
            0.43,
            0.441
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_9_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.691,
            0.068,
            0.891,
            0.282
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_9_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.455,
            0.315,
            0.836,
            0.353
          ],
          "content": "This is true no matter where point A is on line l."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.46,
            0.681,
            0.517
          ],
          "content": "So, finding point A that gives the shortest possible path $\\mathrm { B \\rightarrow } \\mathrm { A \\rightarrow } \\mathrm { C }$ is the same as finding a point A that gives the shortest possible path $\\mathrm{B} \\to \\mathrm{A} \\to \\mathrm{C}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.516,
            0.68,
            0.572
          ],
          "content": "But the solution to the latter problem is clear — choose A on the straight line $$\\mathrm { B } \\mathbb { C } \\mathrm {  勺  } ,$$ since $\\mathrm { ~ B C ~ } _ { 1 } ^ { \\prime }$ is the shortest possible path from B to $\\mathsf { C } ^ { \\prime }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.696,
            0.395,
            0.891,
            0.607
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_9_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.571,
            0.679,
            0.61
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, this triangle ∆ABC has the minimum perimeter."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.618,
            0.729,
            0.641
          ],
          "content": "？ Analyse whether A lies on the perpendicular bisector of BC."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.654,
            0.294,
            0.676
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
